    "media:imagetext": "-4-\nthe waves of conquerors down through the ages. The fact that it\nwasn't robbed was probably due to the nearby construction of more\nimportant burial places in the Valley of the Kings, the material\nfrom which was dumped on top of the tomb of \"Tut\".\nThey have never\nfound the mummy of Cheops and it may be that some future generation\nof archeologists will one day discover that in some corner or part\nof the Great Pyramid built by him.\nKing Tut, as you probably remember,\nwas a very young and unimportant king when he died.\nImagine the mag-\nnificence of old Cheops' secret burial place - if he had one - with\nhis accumulation of priceless Egyptian art objects collected during\nhis thirty years' rule!\nWe had lunch as the guests of our dragomen at an Egyptian\nrestaurant, the feature being lamb roasted over charcoal - very good.\nWe took the afternoon off and in the evening went to dinner at the\nhome of Mr. and Mrs. John Vickers. He is the regional director of\nour U.S. Civil Air Administration and was a passenger with us from\nBangkok to Cairo.\nguests were a Mr. and Mrs. Morse, an el-\nderly couple who also were passengers with us and who run a museum\nin New Hampshire near Franconia, where apparently they have a col-\nlection of\nstuffed animals from Africa plus loot they have picked\nup all over the world.\nThursday\nMarch 24, 1950\nEn route Cairo-Beirut\nOur pilot this time is a Hungarian who told me that the\nRomanian pilot we had on the trip down used to be King Carol's\nThis one said that life behind the iron curtain\nwas\nimpossible, but now he is very happy flying for Middle East. He\nhas just invited Debby to come up to the cockpit to have a good look\nOne of the ancient Pharaohs of\nEgypt planned to dig the Suez Canal but decided against it because\nof the trouble it might cause with the people of the west. How wise\nhe was ! However, as you cover this part of the world you begin to\nunderstand why and how the British maintained their Empire and the peace\nof the world for several centuries. There are British strong points\neverywhere - some of them so small.\nas to not appear on any but the\nsmallest of local maps. Such strategic points as the island which\nblocks the entrance to\nthe Red Sea, several small, widely-spaced\nislands along the coast of Arabia, Bahrein Island in the Persian\nGulf and countless others.\nWith the decline of the British Empire\nwill Uncle Sam carry on the job of world policeman? I'm afraid he\nwon't.\nThe difference is that our foreign markets account for only\nabout 10% of our total trade while with England its foreign trade\nis perhaps 90% and thus while nine out of ten Britishers are deeply\nconcerned with us, only one out of ten is interested.\nOur last three days in Cairo were spent mostly in and around\nthe city with one day's excursion over the Sahara Desert (by auto -\nnot camel.) to Lake Karoum some fifty miles south southwest of Cairo.\nIn Biblical times the like was seventy feet above sea-level but now\nit is one hundred forty feet below and only bout half its former\nsize.\nThe old lake bottom is the most fertile land in Egypt and\nproduces the finest cotton, grapes, figs and dates. We had lunch at",
